module gunbc.live_deploy.candidate_identity

import std.types { CommitSha }
import std.content_hash { Fnv1a64Structural }

// THE PER-INVOCATION RELEASE, WHICH THE SLOT SPEC IS NOT. DeploymentSpec answers WHERE a deployment
// is placed — which instance root, which port, which unit name, which host — and every one of those
// facts is stable across deploys of blue or of green. CandidateRelease answers WHAT is being
// installed on this invocation, and every one of ITS facts changes on every deploy. Fusing them
// would put a mutable revision inside the static slot topology, which is why deployment_spec_srv1
// takes the revision as a parameter rather than owning it: the slot identity stays a constant of
// the fleet model and the release travels beside it.\n\nTWO MEMBERS BECAUSE THE PROCESS REPORTS
// TWO. RunningReleaseObservation carries revision and surface_bundle_identity as separate wire
// members precisely because they fail independently, and a candidate that carried only one of them
// could not be compared against the half it omitted. So the candidate is the EXPECTATION and the
// observation is the READING, member for member, and readiness is the equality between them.

// OBSERVED ONCE, AT THE PUBLIC LAUNCHER, BEFORE ANY HOST MUTATION — and the placement is the whole
// property. A revision rediscovered deeper in the chain (in the emitter, in readiness, in a retry
// arm) is a SECOND observation of a tree that the deploy itself may have changed underneath, so the
// unit could be emitted with one revision and verified against another, and every comparison
// downstream would still look internally consistent. Observing once means the launcher's reading is
// the only revision in the invocation and every later use is a reference to that one value, which
// is what makes 'the checkout's sha, the emitted launch sha, and the process-reported sha are
// equal' a claim with a single subject rather than three readings that happen to agree.\n\nIT IS
// THE DEPLOYING CHECKOUT, NOT A SLOT'S DISK HEAD. The distinction is the one the operator ruling
// turns on. This observation runs in the CI workspace that is performing the deploy, and answers
// 'which candidate am I installing' — a fact the deploying job is the only party that knows.
// Reading a git HEAD inside a slot tree ON THE HOST would answer a different question ('what does
// that directory contain'), and using it as the ACTIVE predecessor is exactly the disk-derived
// ordering the ruling struck down, because the disk diverges from the running process whenever a
// sync is interrupted or lands out of order. Observing the candidate here is safe for the same
// reason that one is not: nothing has been mutated yet, and the checkout is the deploying job's own
// workspace.\n\nTHE SURFACE HALF IS OBSERVED ONCE, WITH THE REVISION. expected_surface_identity and
// expected_dispatch_digest are taken from the one live surface bundle materialized at this
// observation (tree plus the runtime task roster as it is then). They are not a pure function of
// the tree alone: since the live route joined the roster, a roster change is a different surface.
// A roster change between observation and readback is a loud mismatch that a retry resolves, never
// an admission of a recomputed expectation.\n\nTHE SURFACE HALF IS CONSUMED, NOT MERELY
// PUBLISHED. apply.dag carries this whole value into the readiness poll and the digest readback.
// Readiness compares the process-reported surface_bundle_identity against expected_surface_identity;
// the digest readback compares the served dispatch body against expected_dispatch_digest. Neither
// re-materializes the bundle at comparison time. That distinction is the point: a recomputed
// expectation would describe the tree (and roster) AS THEY ARE WHEN COMPARED, so if the deploying
// checkout or roster changed after admission the expectation and the verdict would drift together
// and agree with a process other than the one admitted.

type CandidateRelease {
  revision: CommitSha
  expected_surface_identity: Fnv1a64Structural
  expected_dispatch_digest: Fnv1a64Structural
}
